structure.pattern_description:
IIA states: if option X is optimal in a set S, then X must remain optimal in any subset of S that still contains X. The Minimax Regret criterion breaches IIA because regret is measured relative to the best payoff available in each state. Adding or removing an unchosen alternative changes that benchmark, which can reverse the ordering of the remaining alternatives.

**Full computation**
- Acts: [Live Music, Discount Ale, Board‑Games Night]
- States: [Busy, Slow, Festival]
- Payoffs (gold):
  • LM|Busy=25, LM|Slow=10, LM|Festival=18
  • DA|Busy=20, DA|Slow=12, DA|Festival=22
  • BG|Busy=15, BG|Slow=8,  BG|Festival=24
- Regret with all three options (max per state):
  • Busy max=25 → regrets {LM:0, DA:5, BG:10}
  • Slow max=12 → regrets {LM:2, DA:0, BG:4}
  • Festival max=24 → regrets {LM:6, DA:2, BG:0}
- Max regrets: LM=6, DA=5, BG=10 → Minimax Regret picks DA.
- After removing BG, new max per state:
  • Busy max=25 → regrets {LM:0, DA:5}
  • Slow max=12 → regrets {LM:2, DA:0}
  • Festival max=22 → regrets {LM:4, DA:0}
- New max regrets: LM=4, DA=5 → Minimax Regret now picks LM.
Thus the recommendation flips when an irrelevant alternative (BG) disappears, violating IIA.

